A whole class of frameworks is configured by CONSTRUCTING an object
rather than decorating or subclassing one (app = FastAPI(),
Base = declarative_base(), runner = CliRunner()), and a YAML author had
no key to write a pattern against. Python now stamps the callee on the
bound variable, qualification intact, so a namespaced callee stays
distinguishable from a same-named local.

THE ITEM'S DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT CHANGED THE FIX'S MODULE.
It reported the matcher "lacks call/instantiates/constructor_call entry
families". The matcher has had a call surface since 2026-01-30 —
UsagePatternSpec matches UsageContext on (kind, name, position) — four
months before the filing. But ADR-3ccc models "a first-party symbol
appears INSIDE a framework call", and every position it defines is an
input slot; WI-nopod's cases invert that, since the symbol to tag is the
call's RESULT. The item's own alternative ("~7,939 instantiates edges
already exist") fails for a related reason: measured, those are anchored
at the FILE and the variable carried no link to the callee at all. Both
proposed fixes matched nothing because the binding was never recorded.

Chosen home is Symbol.meta, mirroring meta.base_classes — both record
what type a symbol relates to, as metadata a linker can later promote to
edges. The decisive property: THE MATCHER NEEDS NO CHANGE, since
meta_match already takes a {key: regex} map over Symbol.meta. Asserted by
test, not inferred. Each alternative costs an ADR ruling: a
result-position UsageContext widens symbol_ref and adds a fifth pattern
category to an ADR already marked Implemented; a new edge type is
ADR-0023's territory.

Narrow in three ways, each guarded by a test: only a call result is
stamped, so the key stays useful as a filter; factory and constructor
calls are treated identically, because static analysis cannot
distinguish them and claiming otherwise would be a guess; a computed
callee (factories[key]()) records NOTHING rather than a partial name,
since a YAML matching a fiction would fail silently.

Found while testing: meta_match applies regex.match, not search, so
(^|\.)FastAPI$ silently fails on fastapi.FastAPI while (.*\.)?FastAPI$
works. Pinned with an explicit tripwire.

docs/schema.json regenerated for the new registered meta key.

